'''''Sant Jorge, Tramuntana''''' ({{lang|ca|Sant Jorge}}) is a town in the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca of [[Tramuntana Province]], [[Balboa]]. With a population of 12,204 (2026 estimate), it is the largest settlement in the comarca. Sant Jorge is located in a valley in the northern highlands of Tramuntana Province, along the Sant Jorge River on the Shendan-facing slope of the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range|Yalabirga]] range, north of [[Lake Iwarakí]]. The [[E8 expressway (Balboa)|E8 expressway]] passes through the town, providing a road link across the northern highlands. Founded as a Volisanian trading post in 1625, the settlement remained a small agricultural town until the construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]], a major hydroelectric dam on the southern shore of Lake Iwarakí, in the 1970s. Operated by the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB), the dam became the town's primary employer and transformed the local economy. The [[Sant Jorge Airport]], constructed in 1928, connects the town to the national air network via [[Portblanc Airport|Portblanc]] on the [[Aeroístmus]] route from [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport|Sant Cristòfor]].

== Geography ==

Sant Jorge is situated in a valley in the northern highlands of Tramuntana Province, along the Sant Jorge River on the western (Shendan) slope of the Yalabirga range. The river, a tributary of the Yalabirga watershed, carved the valley in which the town is built, providing one of the few areas of level ground in the northern highlands and a reliable water source that made the site attractive for settlement. The surrounding terrain is rugged, with steep ridges and forested slopes rising above the valley floor.

The climate is cooler and drier than the coastal lowlands, with a pronounced wet season from May to November. The surrounding highlands receive approximately 1,000–1,500 mm of rainfall annually, supporting montane forest and limited agriculture in the valley floor. The Shendan-facing slope lies in the rain shadow of the higher Yalabirga peaks to the east, resulting in drier conditions than the Aethiopian side of the range.

[[Lake Iwarakí]] lies approximately 40 kilometres south of the town. The lake, covering an area of 704.6 km², was a natural lake converted into a reservoir with the construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] dam at its southern outflow. The Sant Jorge River flows south from the highlands through the town and into the lake, which in turn feeds into the [[Yalabirga River]] via the dam's outflow. The lake's Birú name, Iwarakí, means "clear reflections," referring to the still, mirror-like surface of the lake in dry season conditions.

== History ==

The area around Sant Jorge has been inhabited by the [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] for centuries. The Birú retreated into the northern highlands following the Volisanian conquest of the isthmian coast and maintained communities in relative isolation throughout the colonial period.

A Volisanian trading post was established at the site in 1625, named for [[St. George|Sant Jorge]]. The trading post served as a minor node in the colonial highland trade network, facilitating the exchange of agricultural goods and minerals with the coastal settlements. The settlement remained a small agricultural town throughout the colonial and republican periods, with limited growth and minimal integration into the broader colonial economy. The region's remoteness, difficult terrain, and sparse population meant that colonial authority was limited to occasional visits by provincial officials and clergy. The Birú population maintained a degree of autonomy through the colonial and republican periods, though the expansion of cattle ranching and logging into the highland valleys in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries gradually encroached on traditional lands.

The [[Sant Jorge Airport]] was constructed in 1928, providing the first reliable year-round transport link to the town and replacing the seasonal road access that had previously been the only connection to the provincial network. The airport operates as an essential services destination, receiving subsidies under the national air connectivity programme.

In the 1970s, the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB) began construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] on the southern shore of [[Lake Iwarakí]], approximately 40 kilometres south of Sant Jorge. The dam, completed in the late 1970s, was one of the largest infrastructure projects undertaken in Tramuntana Province during the post-reunification hydroelectric expansion. The project brought an influx of construction workers and engineers to the area, and the subsequent operation of the dam and its hydroelectric station provided stable, skilled employment that transformed Sant Jorge from a small agricultural town into the primary service centre for the La Serra de Llevant comarca. The dam's construction raised the water level of the natural lake, converting it into a reservoir with regulated outflow into the [[Yalabirga River]].

== Economy ==

The economy of Sant Jorge is dominated by the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB), which operates the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] hydroelectric dam and power station on [[Lake Iwarakí]]. CHB is the town's primary employer, providing skilled and semi-skilled positions in dam operation, maintenance, administration, and environmental monitoring. The presence of the CHB facility has made Sant Jorge the economic centre of the La Serra de Llevant comarca, supporting a range of service businesses that cater to the dam workforce and their families.

Agriculture remains an important secondary economic activity. The valley floor and surrounding highland slopes support the cultivation of maize, beans, root vegetables, and coffee, primarily for local consumption and regional sale. The Sant Jorge River provides irrigation for the valley's agricultural land. Small mining operations in the surrounding highlands extract limited quantities of minerals, providing supplementary employment for the local population. The town has a weekly market serving the surrounding rural communities.

The town has a health clinic, a primary school, and a secondary school serving the comarca's southern communities. The municipal council provides basic services, though infrastructure remains limited compared to the provincial average. The provincial government has identified the La Serra de Llevant comarca as a priority area for development assistance under the national rural development programme.

== Indigenous community ==

Sant Jorge is located in proximity to several [[Birú people|Birú]] communities in the northern highlands. The Birú population in the area has raised concerns about land use, environmental impact, and the disruption of cultural sites in connection with development projects. In 2026, proposed construction of a data center facility near the town by [[Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses]] drew sustained opposition from indigenous communities and leftist political parties, highlighting the ongoing tension between infrastructure development and indigenous land rights in the Tramuntana highlands.

== Transport ==

{{main|Sant Jorge Airport}}

[[Sant Jorge Airport]] (IATA: SJD, ICAO: SJIB) is a domestic turboprop-only airport located near the town. The airport is served by [[Aeroístmus]] from [[Portblanc Airport|Portblanc]] as a stop on the route from [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport|Sant Cristòfor]], operating under the national essential services programme. The airport handled approximately 55,000 passengers in 2025.

Road connections to Sant Jorge include the [[E8 expressway (Balboa)|E8 expressway]], which passes through the town and provides the primary road link across the northern highlands. A regional road links Sant Jorge to [[Sant Martí del Nord, Tramuntana|Sant Martí del Nord]], the capital of the neighbouring [[La Taula]] comarca, to the northwest. The E8 also provides a route south to [[Portblanc]], the provincial capital. Roads in the area are narrow and winding, reflecting the rugged highland terrain, and are frequently affected by landslides during the wet season.

